course: books
lesson: 71
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter XII, part 2: The Return of Ulysses (four Heroes, five minutes and clean sheets)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: What a squealing and squeaking fills the hall! Weasels dive under the tables and leap for the windows; ferrets rush for the fireplace and get stuck in the chimney; chairs tip over and china crashes. In stride the four friends: Badger with his great stick, Mole shouting his war-cry «A Mole! A Mole!», Rat with his belt bulging with weapons, and Toad puffed up to twice his size, whooping «I'll pleasure 'em!» and going straight for the Chief Weasel.
explain: There are only four of them, but to the panicking weasels the hall seems full of monsters. They flee through the windows, up the chimney, anywhere away from those sticks. In five minutes the hall is empty except for about a dozen weasels lying on the floor, whom Mole handcuffs. Mole goes out to check on the stoat sentries; Badger wants supper, and Toad, a little hurt that nobody praises him, hunts out cold chicken, trifle and lobster salad.
explain: Mole comes back with an armful of rifles. The stoats, already jumpy from his morning trick, heard the uproar; some threw down their guns and ran, and the rest thought the weasels had betrayed them. Stoats and weasels wrestled and rolled about until most of them rolled into the river!
explain: Badger trusts Mole with one more job: march the prisoners upstairs and make them clean the bedrooms properly, with clean sheets, hot water and fresh soap. Mole does it, and lets the sorry weasels go home with a bread roll each. Then Toad, putting away his jealousy, thanks Mole warmly, and Badger says «There spoke my brave Toad!» They sleep between clean sheets in Toad's own house, «won back by matchless valour, consummate strategy, and a proper handling of sticks».
fact: «Well might the terrified weasels dive under the tables and spring madly up at the windows! Well might the ferrets rush wildly for the fireplace and get hopelessly jammed in the chimney!»
fact: Mole's war-cry was «A Mole! A Mole!»; Toad was «swollen to twice his ordinary size, leaping into the air and emitting Toad-whoops» (emitting = letting out).
fact: Toad yelled «Toad he went a-pleasuring!... I'll pleasure 'em!» and went straight for the Chief Weasel; later he is proud that he sent him «flying across the table».
fact: «They were but four in all, but to the panic-stricken weasels the hall seemed full of monstrous animals, grey, black, brown and yellow» (panic-stricken = filled with sudden fear).
fact: «The affair was soon over»: in five minutes the room was cleared; about a dozen enemies lay on the floor, and Mole fitted them with handcuffs.
fact: Badger: «Mole, you're the best of fellows! Just cut along outside and look after those stoat-sentries of yours» (cut along = hurry off).
fact: Badger: «I want some grub, I do» — the book calls this «that rather common way he had of speaking». Looking down on a country way of talking is an old snobbish idea; every dialect is fine.
fact: Supper: guava jelly, cold chicken, a tongue «that had hardly been touched», trifle, lobster salad, French rolls, cheese, butter and celery.
fact: Toad «felt rather hurt that the Badger didn't say pleasant things to him, as he had to the Mole».
fact: Mole's report: the jumpy stoats heard the uproar; some «threw down their rifles and fled»; the rest «thought they were betrayed», and stoats and weasels «wrestled and wriggled... till most of 'em rolled into the river!»
fact: Badger: «I wouldn't trouble you only I know I can trust you to see a thing done... I'd send Rat, if he wasn't a poet.»
fact: The prisoners must make the bedrooms comfortable: «sweep under the beds... clean sheets and pillow-cases... a can of hot water, and clean towels, and fresh cakes of soap». Mole reports every room «as clean as a new pin».
fact: Mole did not punish them: «they had had licking enough for one night». The weasels were «very penitent» (truly sorry), blamed the Chief Weasel and the stoats, and Mole «gave them a roll a-piece».
fact: Toad: «Thank you kindly, dear Mole, for all your pains and trouble tonight, and especially for your cleverness this morning!» Badger: «There spoke my brave Toad!»
fact: «Won back by matchless valour, consummate strategy, and a proper handling of sticks.» (valour = bravery; consummate = perfect; strategy = a clever plan)
fact: Talk in this lesson: «it's giving main character» = acting like the hero; «craic» (Ireland) = fun; «sweet as» (New Zealand) = great; «howay» (North East England) = come on; «chuffed» (UK) = pleased.
exercise: What did the terrified weasels do first? | Dived under tables and sprang at the windows | Fought bravely to the last weasel | Surrendered at once and handed over the keys of Toad Hall | Ran down the secret passage
exercise: Where did the ferrets get stuck? | In the chimney | Under the table | In the doorway, all at once, with the stoats | In the pantry
exercise: What was Mole's war-cry? | «A Mole! A Mole!» | «Charge!» | «Learn 'em!» | «For Toad and Toad Hall!»
exercise: How big did Toad seem? | Swollen to twice his ordinary size | As small as a mouse | His normal size | Bigger than Badger, as tall as the door
exercise: «I'll pleasure 'em!» Which words was Toad throwing back at the weasels? | Their song «Toad he went a-pleasuring» | Badger's orders | The Rat's poem about ducks | The Clerk's sentence
exercise: Whom did Toad go straight for? | The Chief Weasel | The Sergeant | A ferret | The biggest stoat, who held the door
exercise: How many friends were fighting? | Four | Forty | A hundred | Six boatloads, as Mole had said
exercise: Why did the weasels think the hall was «full of monstrous animals»? | They were panic-stricken | There really were hundreds | Mirrors | Otter came too
exercise: How did the weasels escape? | Through the windows, up the chimney, anywhere | Down the passage | By the front door, politely, one after another, saying goodnight | In boats
exercise: How long did the battle take? | About five minutes | All night | An hour | A whole day, till the next morning's breakfast
exercise: What did Mole do to the dozen weasels left on the floor? | Fitted them with handcuffs | Sent them home | Gave them supper | Took them to the police-station in town
exercise: What job did Badger give Mole straight after the battle? | Go out and see what the stoat-sentries were doing | Cook supper | Write invitations to a banquet | Mend the windows
exercise: «I want some grub, I do.» What is «grub» here? | Food | A little worm | Dirt and mud | A small boat
exercise: Why did Toad feel hurt? | Badger praised Mole but said nothing nice to him | Badger hit him by mistake | He lost the fight | He was hungry
exercise: Which of these was NOT found for supper? | Roast beef | Guava jelly | Lobster salad | Trifle in a bowl
exercise: What did Mole carry in through the window? | An armful of rifles | A weasel | Flowers from the garden for the supper-table | Firewood
exercise: Why did the stoats fight the weasels? | They thought they had been betrayed | They were hungry and wanted the weasels' supper | They were bored | Badger told them to
exercise: Where did most of the stoats and weasels end up? | In the river | In prison | In the cellar | Back in the Wild Wood, safe in bed
exercise: Why did Badger send Mole, not Rat, to deal with the prisoners? | He could trust Mole to see a thing done | Rat was hurt | Rat was asleep in an arm-chair after the battle | Rat was too small
exercise: What did the prisoners have to do upstairs? | Clean and tidy the bedrooms | Sing a song of welcome for Toad | Write letters of apology to Toad and all his friends | Cook a big breakfast for everyone
exercise: How clean were the rooms, said Mole? | As clean as a new pin | A bit dusty in the corners | Very dirty, like a pigsty | Clean enough for weasels, but not for a gentleman
exercise: Why didn't Mole give the weasels «a licking»? | They had had licking enough for one night | He was tired | Badger forbade it | They ran away
exercise: Whom did the weasels blame? | The Chief Weasel and the stoats | Toad | Mole | Themselves only, and nobody else at all
exercise: What did Mole give each weasel before letting them go? | A roll | A shilling | A sword | A licking
exercise: «There spoke my brave Toad!» What had Toad just done? | Thanked Mole kindly, putting away his jealousy | Beaten the Chief Weasel | Sung a song about his victory | Written a letter
test: Ava, chuffed (pleased): «the weasels put up a massive long fight, hours and hours.» Put it right. | It was over in about five minutes | Right, it lasted all night until the sun came up | It lasted three days | There was no fight at all
test: Why did four animals seem like a whole army to the weasels? | Panic made them see monsters | There was an army | Toad had brought all his friends from the river | Mirrors doubled them
test: Is it TRUE or FALSE? «Mole's trick with the sentries made no difference.» | False: the stoats thought they were betrayed | True: nothing changed at all | True: the stoats stayed at their posts | False: the stoats joined the friends
test: Ciara, «what's the craic (fun)?»: «Badger called it 'grub', so he's common.» What should we say today? | Every dialect is fine | Yes, he is common | Badger can't speak | Grub is French
test: What would have happened if the weasels had kept their guns at the banquet? | A much harder fight | Nothing different, because sticks beat guns | The weasels would have won easily | Badger would not have come
test: Why does Badger give Mole the most important jobs? | Mole can be trusted | Mole is the biggest and strongest | Mole asked | Badger dislikes Rat
test: «I'd send Rat, if he wasn't a poet.» What is Badger joking about? | Poets are dreamy and might forget the job | Poets are rude | Rat can't climb stairs since he hurt his leg | Poets are strong
test: Jamie, sweet as (great): «Mole was dead kind to the prisoners at the end, eh?» Is he right? | Yes: no licking, a roll each, and he let them go | No: he beat them all and threw them in the river | No: he sent them to prison | Yes: he gave them Toad Hall
test: Why is Toad's thank-you to Mole important? | Toad puts away his jealousy | He wanted more food | Badger made him say it or go without supper | It was a joke
test: Looking back: in chapter III, how did Mole feel alone in the Wild Wood among the faces and the whistling? | Terrified — «the Terror of the Wild Wood» | Brave and calm | Bored, so he went to sleep in a tree | Cheerful
test: Looking back: in chapter XI, what did the grumbling stoats say about the weasels? | The weasels would feast while they froze | That the weasels were brave and good leaders | That the weasels paid well | Nothing
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Badger said the Wild Wood held «good, bad, and indifferent» and «we must all live and let live». How does Mole show that tonight? | He lets the sorry weasels go free with a roll each | He chases them for ever | He keeps them prisoners | He throws them in the river
test: Looking back: in chapter V, Mole felt ashamed of his small, dusty home. What is he doing with Toad's big house now? | Making every room tidy | Selling it to the stoats | Ignoring it | Complaining it is too big
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, what did Rat and Mole do to Toad's motor-clothes? | Took them off him bit by bit | Burnt them on the bonfire | Wore them | Gave them to Badger
test: Looking back: how has Toad changed from chapter XI, when he called Mole «you silly ass»? | He now thanks Mole warmly | He calls him a silly ass again | He ignores him | He wants Mole to leave
test: ★★★ The weasels saw animals «grey, black, brown and yellow». Badger is grey and Mole black. Which two colours are left for the Rat and Toad? | Brown and yellow | Grey and black, the same again | Red and green | Black and white
test: ★★★ «About a dozen» weasels lay on the floor. If Mole had handcuffs for 4 animals each, how many sets did he need for 12? | 3 | 4 | 12 | 48
test: ★★★ «Matchless valour, consummate strategy, and a proper handling of sticks.» Which of the three does Mole's sentry trick show best? | Consummate strategy | Matchless valour in a battle | Handling of sticks | None
exam: Identify the location in which the ferrets became immobilised during the engagement. | The chimney | The butler's pantry | The cellar below | The conservatory
exam: Ascertain the cause of the stoats' conflict with the weasels, according to the Mole's report. | The stoats believed themselves betrayed | The stoats were ordered to do so by the Badger | A quarrel over the banquet | A disagreement concerning their pay
exam: It is given that the Badger entrusted a task to the Mole in preference to the Rat. State the reason he advanced. | That the Rat was a poet | That the Rat was injured | That the Rat was the Mole's junior | That the Rat had overslept
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of the prisoners? | They cleaned the bedrooms and were released | They were conveyed to the police station under escort | They were imprisoned in the cellar | They were thrown into the river
